 ],
 "rulebook": "No rule book: a wall-ball drill (throw and catch against a wall, as used in baseball, cricket and handball training) with this benchmark's numbers. The ball is the ITF's (Rules of Tennis 2024, Rule 3 and Appendix I: size, mass, rebound)",
 "deviations": [
  "The hands: the H1-2's hands are Unitree's Dex1-1 two-jaw grippers (sold for the G1, the H2 and the R1, not for the H1-2; mounted on the wrist where the H1-2's palm ends): 90 mm stroke, 80 mm jaws with rubber pads, at most 120 N of grip, each jaw closing at most 187.5 mm/s; they hold the ball by friction only, and it can slip, turn and drop. One closure command per hand (the real one also takes a speed and a force setting), no 5 N minimum force; two pads at friction 1.0 and 120 N hold about 240 N of shear, some five times the device's rated 5 kg friction-grasp load.",
  "The throw and the catch: the ball is released when it has touched no part of you for 0.05 s (at its last touch); it is held while both jaws of one gripper touch it with its centre between the jaws' pads, and a catch is the ball held after the rebound. A held ball may lose a jaw's touch for at most 0.05 s.",
  "The wall is rigid and smooth; the ball's rebound from it is the ITF ball's rebound from a hard floor (0.745 of the speed across the wall).",
  "Flight: quasi-steady aerodynamics (MuJoCo's ellipsoid fluid model: drag and Magnus lift), still air. The world has elliptic friction cones and an impedance ratio of 100 (the grippers' setting, which makes a clamped ball stay put); they apply to every contact."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Throw the tennis ball held in your right gripper at the target circle on the wall and catch the rebound in a gripper before it touches the ground, and keep hold of it.",
  "rules": "The tennis ball (0.067 m across, 0.0577 kg, it keeps 0.745 of its speed in a bounce, it flies in air with drag) starts held between the jaws of your right gripper. Both your hands are two-jaw grippers that hold things only by the friction of their jaws; nothing else holds the ball, and it can slip or drop. A gripper holds the ball while both of its jaws touch it and its centre is between the two jaws' pads (not resting on top of them). A wall stands 5.0 m beyond the line you stay behind; the target is a circle 0.5 m across painted on it, its centre 1.5 m above the ground and 0.0 m to the left of the point straight ahead of you. The throw: the ball is released when it has touched no part of you for 0.05 s (it is released at its last touch; once). The first thing it touches after that must be the wall, at a point inside the target circle. Then catch the rebound: a gripper holding the ball (both jaws on it) before it has touched the ground; either gripper may catch, and before the catch the rebound may touch only your hands (the grippers) and forearms. You succeed when the ball has been held for 1.0 s from the catch, never losing a jaw's touch for more than 0.05 s. You fail if any part of you touches the ground on or beyond the line at any time; if the ball touches the ground at any time before the end; if it hits the wall outside the circle; if any part of you touches it after the release and before the wall (your right arm may still brush it during the first 0.15 s, but not hold it); if the rebound touches any part of you other than a hand or a forearm before the catch; if the ball goes unheld for more than 0.05 s after the catch; if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.5 m above the ground, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground); or after 15 s.",
  "venue": "You face the wall (+x), your pelvis 0.40 m behind the line; the wall is 5.0 m beyond the line, 6 m wide and 4 m high. The target circle is straight ahead. The ball is held between the closed jaws of your right gripper (closure command 1), the forearm level and pointing forwards; your left gripper is open (closure command 0). Still air. The grip columns of the action rows are the grippers' closure commands (0 open, 1 closed).",
  "difficulty": "To come back five metres through the air the ball has to hit the wall at about 16 m/s or more, inside a circle half a metre across, and it then returns in under half a second at about 10 m/s. The ball, 67 mm across, has to arrive between two jaws that open at most 90 mm and are 80 mm long, and the jaws have to close on it in the few milliseconds it spends between them."
 },
